From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Sun, 10 Jul 1994 08:17:55 -0800
Subject: [#94-7-74] AI development acceleration

Carl Feynman writes:
>How fast a small number of people can advance a technology depends on how
>well-developed the technology is.  In the beginning, there is a lot of
>"low-hanging fruit"-- you just walk around plucking a 10% improvement here
>and a 20% improvement there.  Eventually it gets harder, and you need big
>research and development teams with lots of specialists and subcontractors.
>Look at airplanes before 1910 or computers before 1952: radical
>improvements were made by tiny teams.  As the technology matures, you can
>maintain exponential improvement, but only by throwing huge development
>effort at it.  This is where airplanes were up until about 1970, and
>computers are today.
>
>I was implicitly assuming that there were six orders of magnitude available
>in the "low-hanging fruit" stage of development.  I guess you're right that
>that seems implausible.  I wouldn't be suprised if there were three orders
>of magnitude, though.

I am not sure that "software engineering" fits into this model -- at least
not  that part of software engineering which consists of the development of
new algorithms.  It seems to me that the science of constructing new
algorithms is much more akin to "mathematical research", where I think this
model clearly does not hold.  For example, in the field of cryptography,
the acceleration in our ability to do prime factoring on large composite
numbers (i.e. break RSA) has been almost entirely driven by improvements in
factoring algorithms, which have been made by just one or at most a few
mathematicians working at a time (that is, the improvements due to
algorithmic development have overshadowed hardware advances by several
orders of magnitude).

Some areas of software engineering certainly do fit into Carl's model --
for example, building good speech recognition, etc.  And probably a part of
initial AI development will require this large development team approach.
However, I suggest that the rapid *acceleration* phase might be driven by
algorithmic improvements of the mathematical sort.  For example, in fields
such as neural networks, or genetic engineering, it is at least possible
that even a single researcher in isolation might come up with algorithmic
improvements resulting in orders of magnitude increase in efficiency,
without radical redesign of other portions of the system.

>>  (And I don't see how
>>the AI scenario could "take off" until the AIs reached near human
>>intelligence levels.)
>
>Of course.  At least near-human intelligence at engineering tasks, anyway.

Perhaps what would be required is near-human intelligence at mathematical
research.  Some people (e.g. Penrose) have contended that mathematical
"intuition" may be a qualitatively harder task than, say, mechanical
engineering...

Doug


