From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Sun, 10 Jul 1994 11:56:51 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-76] AI development acceleration

Carl Feynman writes:
>I have been assuming ... the hardware is fixed, but the software keeps
>getting improved by substantial factors.  I think the extraordinary
>rates of progress in hardware, versus the minuscule rates of progress
>in AI (or uploading), will produce quite an overhang by the time we
>finally get an intelligent machine. ...
>
>How fast a small number of people can advance a technology depends on how
>well-developed the technology is.  In the beginning, there is a lot of
>"low-hanging fruit"-- you just walk around plucking a 10% improvement here
>and a 20% improvement there.  Eventually it gets harder, and you need big
>research and development teams with lots of specialists and subcontractors.
>Look at airplanes before 1910 or computers before 1952: radical
>improvements were made by tiny teams.  As the technology matures, you can
>maintain exponential improvement, but only by throwing huge development
>effort at it.  This is where airplanes were up until about 1970, and
>computers are today.
>
>I was implicitly assuming that there were six orders of magnitude available
>in the "low-hanging fruit" stage of development.  I guess you're right that
>that seems implausible.  I wouldn't be suprised if there were three orders
>of magnitude, though.

What Carl describes is a "breakthrough" scenario.  In it, AI comes not
because the computer industry makes machines smarter every year, and
eventually they are as smart as us.  Instead, some conceptual
breakthough occurs, some new approach is found, and then there are a
lot of easy ways to improve initial designs.  

In order for this breakthrough scenario to be combined with Carl's
`AIs design themselves' acceleration scenario, this breakthrough must
happen to make AI abilities jump from some subhuman level to about a
human or level.  If it jumps too low, we're back to standard growth as
we pass the human level.  If it jumps too high, the key was the
breakthrough; the further improvements are just icing on the cake.

Breakthroughs are certainly possible, but my personal opinion is that
we are unlikely to discover any huge new conceptual keys to designing
intelligence - I envision just lots and lots of little improvements.

>> it is hard to take this seriously as a "destabilizing effect on war".
>
>I agree.  I got started on this topic not because I was claiming that AI
>would lead to war, but because someone said that an AI runaway was
>impossible, based on analogy to existing technology.

This discussion started with George Sullivan suggesting that we
consider "international governmental controls" on technologies where a
small group might make such rapid progress that they would be tempted
to take over the world militarily.  So I think we should distinguish
"world runaway" from "small group runaway" scenarios.  In the former,
world technology/wealth might increase by several orders of magnitude
in a short time; in the latter, this increase would be confined to a
single military power.  Which are you arguing for?

>In such a scenario, an AI would be different from an upload in that it
>would probably be easier to redesign and improve an AI without breaking it,
>since we knew how to design it in the first place.

Perhaps, but upload technology seems more certain to be a
breakthrough, as it is more intrinsically discrete a transition.  One
day you don't have a working upload, another day you do.  With AI, one
day you have a dumber AI, and another day you have a smarter one.

Doug Cutrell writes:
>For example, in the field of cryptography, the acceleration in our
>ability to do prime factoring on large composite numbers (i.e. break
>RSA) has been almost entirely driven by improvements in factoring
>algorithms, which have been made by just one or at most a few
>mathematicians working at a time (that is, the improvements due to
>algorithmic development have overshadowed hardware advances by several
>orders of magnitude).

Crytography seems much more subject to big breakthroughs to me, more
like physics where lots of work can be boiled down to a few essential
equations.  And note that it wasn't the same few people making these
breakthroughs, so the idea of acceleration by a small isolated team
isn't supported here.

To me the most plausible rapid transition scenario, for AIs or
uploads, comes from the ability to make copies of software fast.  Once
something is created which is more efficient than large numbers of
ordinary human workers, copies can be rapidly made to replace those
human workers.  The military scenario (which I don't find especially
plausible) is you have 10 million robot soldier bodies in storage, and
one day you get smart software to put in those bodies.

Robin Hanson



