From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sun, 10 Jul 94 22:17:39 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-78] AI development acceleration (was: Nanotechnology and uncertainty)

Carl Feynman:
> Note that this [runaway AI] scenario is independent of the level of
> nanotechnology available at the time of the development of AI.

Absolutely.  Nanotech is irrelevant to this interesting topic.

Robin Hanson:
> Maybe designing intelligence is so damn hard that smarter creatures
> than us find it even harder to improve on themselves

Maybe, but I think that constraints on intelligence are going to be more
hardware limited than design/software limited.  I say this from the
perspective of thinking, as an amateur, that neural nets are the way
forward.  There are some large conceptual gaps in our understanding of
neural nets - such as finding a local learning algorithm for randomly
connected networks and how to add new nodes to an operational network
- but I think they are likely to be solved by the time the hardware is
up to providing a platform for human-level AIs, say by about 2030-2040.

If hardware is the driving technology behind the transition from merely
human-level AI to godlike AIs then it is likely to be a gradual global
phenomenon rather than focused on a single, secretive lab.  The trend
in chip fabrication advances is towards more collaborative joint
ventures to cover the investment required.  If this is correct then the
development of godlike AIs is going to be a highly visible capital
market activity, although it may take place over only a couple of years,
as Carl envisages.

Driven by the investment from the capital markets I think the speed-
up/runaway scenario is more likely to follow from the economic
consequences of AIs (as Robin said) as they replace human labour, rather
than the lab-working-in-secret view.  When AIs are capable of replacing
humans then the dividends will be apparent to all investors and funding
will really go through the roof.

> We don't really understand intelligence well enough to say one way or
> the other

True, we can't be sure, but do we have any reason to suspect that
intelligence is anything other than a function of neural complexity? 
I can't think of anything convincing.

> and the overoptimism of previous estimates of rates of AI
> progress is not especially encouraging.

Certainly true.  The AI field has, over the decades, lost a lot of
credibility through rash prognostications.  Estimates for AI based on
notions of complexity (as in human = approx 10^17 bits/sec) and economic
cost of replacing human labour seem more realistic, though, than
previous crystal ball gazing.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

