From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 94 09:13:25 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-82] AI development acceleration (was: Nanotechnology and uncertainty)

I asked:
> ...do we have any reason to suspect that intelligence 
> is anything other than a function of neural complexity? 

Robin Hanson:
> Yes!  A random "big complex" network is almost certainly really dumb.

When I said "randomly connected" I only meant in contrast with the
simpler forward-feed networks, which are clearly dumb.  We need
circularity, which is implied by randomly connected, for intelligence,
amongst other properties.

> Intelligence requires the right *kind* of complexity - not just any
> complexity will do.

My hope is that with some as yet unknown local learning rule that the
right kind of complexity will emerge as a spontaneous order, within the
small randomly connected network as it develops into a bigger
"intelligently connected" network.

I envisage that the local learning rule will dictate things like
snipping connections if they stablise at small interconnect strengths
and inserting extra nodes between connections if the interconnect values
fail to stablise.  Thus isolated nodes are discarded and new nodes form
in the more dynamic areas.  In a stimulating environment the network
would grow (I hope) and develop complexity.  Also nodes with the same
external connections and strengths could be merged with no loss of
information.  Another idea is that nodes with sustained correlated
firing could form interconnections.  The odd random connection forming
might produce interesting effects.

Lots of ideas are waiting to be explored.  The limiting thing is
searching through the learning algorithm space.  This I expect to be
very computational expensive, since we can't look at an algorithm and
deduce which ones are the winners and which are dead-ends, beyond a
certain point.  The only way to test them is the run them on networks
and observe their behaviour.  Another number sink is that the dynamic
networks I'm imagining would very likely have to be run as simulations
to cope with the dynamic rewiring required.  Small networks with
different learning algorithms could be tested against each other
different environments and genetic algorithms used to evolve superior
learning rules.

Obviously this is just speculation, but the point is that there seem
plenty of ways of moving forward if only we had the number-crunching
power available, which is what I mean by AI research being hardware
limited, rather than software limited.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

