Date: Mon, 11 Jul 1994 08:08:57 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-83] emergence defined

 At Alife 4, Vince Darley (vince@das.harvard.edu) proposed this definition:
"A true emergent phenomenon is one for which the optimal means of prediction
is simulation".
 He also proposed a measure of emergence, the "emergence ratio" u(n)/s(n),
where s(n) is the amount of computation needed to predict the behavior of
the system via simulation, and u(n) is the amount of computation needed to
predict the system from a theoretical understanding of it. "Amount of
computation needed" is defined in terms of the Kolmogorov complexity of
the optimum representations ("God's simulation" and "God's understanding"),
so this is a bit too idealized to be accurately measured, but I hope that
it will help to counter the abuse of the concept by mystics and similarly
misguided people.
