From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Mon, 11 Jul 1994 08:26:27 -0800
Subject: [#94-7-85] AI development acceleration

Robin Hanson writes:

>Crytography seems much more subject to big breakthroughs to me, more
>like physics where lots of work can be boiled down to a few essential
>equations.  And note that it wasn't the same few people making these
>breakthroughs, so the idea of acceleration by a small isolated team
>isn't supported here.

And Mike Price writes:

>My hope is that with some as yet unknown local learning rule that the
>right kind of complexity will emerge as a spontaneous order, within the
>small randomly connected network as it develops into a bigger
>"intelligently connected" network...
>...Obviously this is just speculation, but the point is that there seem
>plenty of ways of moving forward if only we had the number-crunching
>power available, which is what I mean by AI research being hardware
>limited, rather than software limited.

I agree with Mike Price's first point, that single breakthroughs of the
algorithmic sort (local learning rules, self-organizational principles,
etc.) may be a driving force in AI acceleration.  This is why I previously
argued that the development of AI may follow the model of mathematical
research more than that of an engineering project.

For the same reason, I would tend to agree with Carl Feynman, that AI
development, in the *final* stages, may indeed be driven by improvements in
software, not hardware.  However, I see these software advances as being of
the purely algorithmic sort... better self-organizational principles, etc.

Mathematical breakthroughs do usually proceed from just one or a few
mathematicians at a time.  As Robin Hanson indicates, often there are
multiple teams, each contributing some piece to the overall puzzle.  But
there are also examples where a single researcher has single-handedly, and
unexpectedly, sown up an entire field of inquiry.  (The work of Michael
Aschbacher towards the classification of all finite simple groups comes to
mind...)  And if some early AI is better at developing new
self-organizational principles than humans, then we might see the rapid
exponential explosion that Carl Feynman has outlined.

While this debate is still quite interesting, I think it is clear that at
some point we are faced with simple differences of subjective feeling as to
the qualitative nature of likely AI intelligence.  I see there being at
least two major camps... those who see AI as requiring the gradual piecing
together of a very complicated system, with many individually crafted
components, and those who see AI as potentially arising emergently from a
much more undifferentiated complex system.

Doug


